store, worker: forget a blob whose file is gone, so it is fetched again

Applies to NativeLink v1.8.6 (37f3b206a8e6d55613abae2b18a0665fff24e096).
build-worker.sh applies it on macOS, after the clone patch, and on Linux by
itself; ../../README.md, "Version and patches", says where each binary runs.

A worker's filesystem store answers `has()` from its index. When an entry's
file is no longer on disk the index still lists the blob, so
`populate_fast_store` takes it for resident and fetches nothing. v1.8.6
repairs an input whose blob was evicted while its tree was staged by fetching
it and resolving it again, up to three times; with a stale entry every one of
those fetches is a no-op, and the action fails. So does every later action
that needs the blob, until the worker restarts and rebuilds the index from
the directory. An input that every test shares takes the whole pool's tests
down with it: the worker fails each of them at once, is free again
immediately, and is handed the retries.

What an action reports:

  executable-variant copy failed: Os { code: 2, kind: NotFound, ... } :
  Resolving executable variant for <digest>

for an executable input, whose 0o555 copy is made from the blob, and `Could
not make hardlink ... file was likely evicted from cache` for a plain one.

Seen on 2026-11-20 on a v1.6.6 worker, where a race in eviction left such an
entry for a 941-byte script that every sandboxed test stages: 31 of 95 Linux
CI jobs failed over seven hours. v1.8.6 closes that race (a file's path
carries a generation, so a late removal cannot take a newer file), and a
read through the store already drops a stale entry. The two paths the worker
stages inputs by did not:

- Building an executable variant. The copy's `NotFound`, when it is the
  source that is gone, is now reported as `NotFound` instead of `Internal`,
  and the entry is dropped. `NotFound` is what the worker's fetch-and-resolve
  loop retries on, and the retry now fetches.
- Linking a plain input again after the batch found its source gone. A
  `NotFound` from that link drops the entry (`forget_if_missing`), for the
  same reason.

An entry is dropped only when its own file is missing, looked at under the
entry's path lock, and only that entry: one inserted since has a file of its
own. Tests: the store drops a stale entry when a variant cannot be built and
leaves an entry alone whose file exists; the worker stages a tree whose plain
and executable inputs both sit behind stale entries. Without the fix the
worker test fails with the first message above.

Not upstream. Drop this file when a release clears a stale entry on these two
paths.

